l implementations. The constraint is clear: if source code contains .go files for a package, I shouldn't propose recreating it. The source code takes precedence over what the documentation claims about status.\n\nSo all the release 00.0 use cases either fall outside the scope or target packages that already have substantial code. None of them are actually viable to propose. Looking at the requirements for each package, I'm verifying that the implementations actually exist in the codebase. For testutils, all five requirements are covered across difftest.go and runner.go—the DiffTest struct, normalizer functions, test execution with timeouts, and output comparison are all there. The sys package has terminal operations and file metadata functions implemented across terminal.go, stat.go, and platform-specific files. And format has the column alignment and ANSI color utilities in place across align.go and color.go.\n\nBut wait—the code_status shows \"not started\" and zero test files, which suggests the implementation code exists but the test suites don't. Since I can only propose one task and it must be for release 00.0, and the implementations are already complete, what's actually missing here are the differential tests themselves. The architecture mentions each package should have <utility>_test.go files with differential tests, so that's likely what needs to be created. tests)\" - but that's for cmd/ packages. For pkg/ packages, the test files would be standard Go unit tests.\n\nThe code_status shows testfiles: 0 for all rel00.0 use cases. So tests are missing.\n\nBut wait - the constraint says \"Do NOT propose tasks for paths excluded by ARCHITECTURE.yaml design decision DD7\". Let me re-read DD7... It says \"The generation pipeline targets only cmd/ and pkg/ directories.\" So pkg/ IS a valid target.\n\nAlso, constraint P2 says: \"Always split implementation and tests into separate tasks.\" Since the implementation already exists, I should propose test tasks.\n\nBut the source_code exists for the packages. The constraint says not to recreate them. Tests for existing packages are NOT recreation - they're adding test coverage.\n\nI don't have the PRDs inline, only the source code and architecture docs, though the PRD IDs are referenced in code comments. Since I can only propose one task, I should focus on the most foundational package - testutils is the differential testing harness that everything else depends on, so testing it first makes sense. I need to examine what the harness actually exposes: the DiffTest struct, RunDiffTests function, and related components that would need unit test coverage. I need to pick one task for release 00.0, and since the implementation code exists but tests don't, I should write tests for testutils—it's foundational and other packages depend on it. For the unit tests themselves, I'd verify that functions like buildEnv correctly merge environment variables, setEnvVar handles overrides, applyNormalizers chains functions in the right order, and TimestampNormalizer works as expected, keeping it within the 150-250 line target.\n\nBut I should double-check the PRD requirements and make sure I'm only proposing tasks that align with the actual release 00.0 scope—let me verify which use cases are actually included. The harness needs actual binaries to test against, so I could use simple shell commands or create minimal Go test helper binaries to verify that the difftest runner correctly executes two programs, compares their outputs, and reports any divergences. Let me sketch out what the test structure should look like.\n\nI can test most of the utility functions directly since they're pure functions—the normalizers, environment builders, byte truncation, and divergence formatting all have straightforward inputs and outputs. For the filesystem and binary execution pieces, I could leverage standard Unix tools like `echo`, `cat`, or `true` to avoid needing custom test binaries.\n\nLooking at the project requirements, the test file should mirror the source structure, so `difftest_test.go` makes sense. Given the 150-250 line constraint and the need to cover the core functionality, I should prioritize testing the runner functions since they're the most critical path. I've settled on four test areas covering the core functionality: normalizer functions, environment construction, divergence formatting, and expected file comparison. But I'm second-guessing whether I should be testing the harness itself versus the tests that use it—the differential testing harness is foundational, so validating it makes sense before other tests depend on it.\n\nDefining the task now: I'll write unit tests for the testutils package, covering requirements R2 through R5 from the product spec. This means testing the DiffTest struct, the normalizer composition system, and the runner logic that orchestrates the differential testing flow. iffTest, runBinary, buildEnv, compareExpectedFiles, formatDivergence)\n\n    files:\n      - path: pkg/testutils/runner_test.go\n        action: create\n        note: Unit tests for runner functions (buildEnv, runBinary, comparison, file checks)\n      - path: pkg/testutils/difftest_test.go\n        action: create\n        note: Unit tests for normalizer functions and DiffTest integration\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Test buildEnv and setEnvVar: verify LC_ALL=C default, environment merging, and variable override behavior (prd001-testutils R2.6)\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Test TimestampNormalizer and ComposeNormalizers: verify timestamp patterns are replaced and normalizer chaining works correctly (prd001-testutils R4.2, R4.4)\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Test formatDivergence and truncateBytes: verify divergence reports include args, stdin, stdout, stderr, exit codes, and that long stdin is truncated (prd001-testutils R3.5)\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Test compareExpectedFiles: verify file content comparison succeeds on match and fails on mismatch or missing file (prd001-testutils R5.1, R5.2)\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Test file naming follows P7: runner_test.go mirrors runner.go, difftest_test.go mirrors difftest.go\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Use t.TempDir() for any filesystem-dependent tests (compareExpectedFiles)\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"Test pure functions directly (buildEnv, applyNormalizers, truncateBytes, formatDivergence) without requiring binary execution\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"go test ./pkg/testutils/... passes with all new tests\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"buildEnv tests verify LC_ALL=C is set by default and caller env vars are merged\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"TimestampNormalizer tests verify all three timestamp patterns are replaced with <TIMESTAMP>\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"compareExpectedFiles tests verify both match and mismatch cases using t.TempDir()\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"No test depends on external binaries or network access\"\n\nNow I'm realizing the unexported functions are accessible within the same package, so testing them directly in runner_test.go and difftest_test.go is valid. The naming convention aligns with P7 requirements. I should verify the line count estimates are reasonable for the scope—roughly 150-250 lines across both files seems manageable for these four requirements while staying within constraints.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":27645,"cache_read_input_tokens":19292,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":27645},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9de969fa-72ec-48e2-bfde-c1519b6a616a","uuid":"904b2879-e596-4063-977c-2c74d270b9c3"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01GnUPHSgatd9vxSCpCpRYoq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem:** Classic Unix utilities lack formal specifications accessible to implementers. This project derives specs from C source, generates Go implementations via AI, and verifies parity through differential testing against GNU reference binaries.\n\n**Architecture:** Four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify). Key components: `cmd/` (per-utility Go binaries), `pkg/testutils/` (differential testing harness), `pkg/sys/` (syscall abstractions), `pkg/format/` (output formatting), `magefiles/` (build targets, excluded from generation).\n\n**Current State:** All three `pkg/` packages have implementation code on the branch (`pkg/testutils/difftest.go` + `runner.go`, `pkg/sys/` stat + terminal files, `pkg/format/` align + color + size files). No test files exist for any package (testfiles: 0 across all rel00.0 use cases). `rel00.0-uc001-magefiles` is excluded by DD7.\n\n**Current Release:** rel00.0 (Batch 0 — foundational infrastructure). Remaining code work: tests for `pkg/testutils/`, `pkg/sys/`, and `pkg/format/`.\n\n## Priority Reasoning\n\nRelease 00.0 has four use cases. uc001-magefiles is excluded (DD7). The remaining three (uc002-format, uc003-sys, uc004-testutils) all have implementations but zero test files. Per P5 (dependency ordering): libraries before consumers, foundational before features. `pkg/testutils/` is the differential testing harness — every other package's tests depend on it working correctly. Testing it first is the highest-priority task.\n\n## Proposed Tasks\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"prd001-testutils R2-R5: Unit tests for differential testing harness\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd001-testutils.yaml (requirements R2, R3, R4, R5)\n      - pkg/testutils/difftest.go (DiffTest struct, NormalizeFunc type, TimestampNormalizer, ComposeNormalizers)\n      - pkg/testutils/runner.go (runSingleDiffTest, runBinary, buildEnv, setEnvVar, applyNormalizers, formatDivergence, truncateBytes, compareExpectedFiles)\n\n    files:\n      - path: pkg/testutils/runner_test.go\n        action: create\n        note: Unit tests for runner.go functions -- buildEnv, setEnvVar, formatDivergence, truncateBytes, compareExpectedFiles\n      - path: pkg/testutils/difftest_test.go\n        action: create\n        note: Unit tests for difftest.go functions -- TimestampNormalizer, ComposeNormalizers, applyNormalizers\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Test buildEnv and setEnvVar: verify LC_ALL=C is applied by default, caller-provided KEY=VALUE pairs are merged on top, and existing variables are overridden rather than duplicated (prd001-testutils R2.6)\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Test TimestampNormalizer and ComposeNormalizers: verify all three timestamp regex patterns (month-day-time, ISO datetime, HH:MM:SS) are replaced with <TIMESTAMP>, and that ComposeNormalizers chains multiple NormalizeFuncs in order (prd001-testutils R4.2, R4.4)\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Test formatDivergence and truncateBytes: verify the divergence report includes args, stdin, ref/go stdout, ref/go stderr, and ref/go exit code fields; verify truncateBytes returns <nil> for nil input, full content for short input, and truncated content with byte count for long input (prd001-testutils R3.5)\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Test compareExpectedFiles: verify file content comparison passes when file matches expected bytes, fails when content differs, and fails when the expected file does not exist (prd001-testutils R5.1, R5.2)\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Test file naming follows project convention: runner_test.go mirrors runner.go, difftest_test.go mirrors difftest.go\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Use t.TempDir() for filesystem-dependent tests (compareExpectedFiles) to ensure cleanup\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"Test unexported pure functions directly (buildEnv, setEnvVar, applyNormalizers, truncateBytes, formatDivergence) -- test files are in package testutils so they have access\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"go test ./pkg/testutils/... passes with zero failures\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"buildEnv tests verify LC_ALL=C is present in output, a caller-provided variable appears in output, and overriding an existing variable replaces rather than duplicates it\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"TimestampNormalizer tests cover all three patterns: 'Feb 19 12:34:56' (month-day-time), '2026-02-19 12:34:56' (ISO), and '12:34:56' (time-only) are each replaced with '<TIMESTAMP>'\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"compareExpectedFiles tests create temporary files via t.TempDir() and verify match, mismatch, and missing-file cases\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"No test depends on external binaries, network access, or specific host environment beyond the Go toolchain\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":27645,"cache_read_input_tokens":19292,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":27645},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9de969fa-72ec-48e2-bfde-c1519b6a616a","uuid":"5644b293-5d80-46c4-98eb-b12eda09d7b5"}
